#: src/semantic-tokens.md:101
msgid "Example: Using Theme Styles"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:103
msgid "Instead of hardcoding colors, reference styles from your theme:"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:110 src/semantic-tokens.md:112
msgid "\"variable\""
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:111
msgid "\"mutable\""
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:112
msgid "\"style\""
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:112
msgid "\"variable.mutable\""
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:119
msgid ""
"The first style found in the current theme is used, providing fallback "
"options."
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:121
msgid "Example: Disabling a Token Type"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:123
msgid ""
"To disable highlighting for a specific token type, add an empty rule that "
"matches it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:130
msgid "\"comment\""
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:137
msgid ""
"Since user rules are prepended to defaults and the first match wins, this "
"empty rule prevents any styling from being applied to comment tokens."
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:139
msgid "Default Rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:141
msgid ""
"Zed's default semantic token rules map standard LSP token types to common "
"theme styles. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:143
msgid "`function` → `function` style"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:144
msgid "`variable` with `constant` modifier → `constant` style"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:145
msgid "`class` → `type.class`, `class`, or `type` style (first found)"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:146
msgid ""
"`comment` with `documentation` modifier → `comment.documentation` or "
"`comment.doc` style"
msgstr ""

#: src/semantic-tokens.md:148
msgid ""
"The full default configuration can be shown in Zed with the `zed: show "
"default semantic token rules` command."
msgstr ""

